|  For example, Lynch (1997) studied the judge (in criminal court) as
|  a figure within the collaborative production of a courtroom hearing ...
|  (p. 99). Lynch followed Garfinkel, who transforms Durkheim’s (1938)
|  classic aphorism, ‘the objective reality of social facts is sociology’s fundamental phenomenon,’ by setting out an ethnomethodological alternative
|  to Durkheim’s analytic sociology ...\ [which is] to examine the regularities ...\ [of social life] ...\ and to explicate how social order is achieved
|  reflexively in and through the unrelenting practices of local parties who
.  dwell in a society (p. 100)


--- Sentence/switch
