p: .
t. 
|  As protective mothers become increasingly entrenched in their family court cases and they come to see what is happening to them as wholly
|  inexplicable - losing custody and visitation, sometimes going to jail for
|  reasons that are never properly indicated on the court record - they
|  engage in methods of practical reasoning that enable them to convert
|  these unique and bizarre events into a socially meaningful typification or
|  mold, which can be recognized as a type or an instance of  rather than
|  the only one of its kind. These acts of practical reasoning can take various forms, often influenced by the mother’s social characteristics such
.  as education, class, and race

