p: .
t. 
|  Such opposing projections of how a judge would assess maternal
|  fitness are most sharply apparent in those early interviews between
|  mothers and CPS workers in cases in which the mother believes a child
|  has been sexually abused and before litigation has commenced. What
|  commonly occurs is that a mother, in her effort to prove to the caseworker that the child is in danger of continuing abuse by the father,
|  gives graphic details of the abuse, speaking with urgency and alarm,
|  hoping the caseworker’s report will persuade a judge to protect her child
|  from the abuse. The caseworker, however, may be likely to project thejudge as one who will seriously question the mother’s parental fitness
|  for not preventing such abuse from occurring in the first place or for
|  displaying an obsessive concern with her suspicions. Here is where
|  the contrasting projections of court participants can play easily into the
|  madness of the courts - for the mother’s presentation of the urgency of her
|  situation can result in her being made the target of a CPS neglect
|  charge for failure to protect her child from abuse or for obsessive
.  or alienating behavior

