|  We can thus begin to see how the application of the ethnomethodological form of sociological inquiry to the family court system provides special insight into how judges and their auxiliaries produce
|  and manage social organization as a situated achievement. By applying
|  ethnomethodology, we learn how both judges and protective mothers
|  organize their situated practices and, in so doing, re-create the judicial madness and parental mutiny that have come to characterize the
